| Detection Metadata                                                                    |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Detection Name    : Suspicious PowerShell Execution                                   |
| Detection ID      : DET-001                                                           |
| Author            : SOC Detection Lab v2                                              |
| MITRE Tactic      : Execution                                                         |
| MITRE Technique   : T1059.001 — Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell         |
| Severity          : High                                                              |
| Data Sources      : Sysmon Event ID 1, Windows Event ID 4104                         |
| Splunk Index      : index=endpoint                                                    |
| Status            : Validated                                                         |

================================================================================
DETECTION DESCRIPTION
================================================================================

This detection identifies PowerShell executions that exhibit characteristics
commonly associated with malicious or suspicious use:

  - Encoded command execution (-EncodedCommand / -enc)
  - Execution policy bypass (-ExecutionPolicy Bypass / -ep bypass)
  - Hidden window style (-WindowStyle Hidden / -w hidden)
  - Download cradles (DownloadString, DownloadFile, WebClient, Invoke-WebRequest)
  - In-memory execution (IEX, Invoke-Expression)
  - Unusual parent processes (Office apps, script engines, browsers)
  - Non-interactive (non-console) invocation methods

Adversaries frequently use these flags to run obfuscated or downloaded
payloads while avoiding user visibility and bypassing default execution policies.

================================================================================
DATA REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

Required for this detection to function:

  1. Sysmon installed with Event ID 1 (Process Creation) enabled
     - Requires CommandLine logging enabled in sysmonconfig.xml
  2. Windows Script Block Logging enabled (Event ID 4104)
     - GPO: Computer Config > Admin Templates > Windows Components >
       Windows PowerShell > Turn on PowerShell Script Block Logging
  3. Splunk Universal Forwarder collecting from:
     - Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational
     - Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational

Splunk sourcetypes expected:
  - XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational
  - XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational

================================================================================
PRIMARY SPL QUERY — Sysmon Event ID 1 (Process Creation)
================================================================================

| Detection: Suspicious PowerShell via Command Line Flags |

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational"
EventID=1
Image IN ("*\\powershell.exe", "*\\pwsh.exe")
(
    CommandLine IN (
        "*-EncodedCommand*",
        "*-enc *",
        "*-ExecutionPolicy Bypass*",
        "*-ep bypass*",
        "*-ExecutionPolicy bypass*",
        "*-WindowStyle Hidden*",
        "*-w hidden*",
        "*-NonInteractive*",
        "*-noni *",
        "*IEX*",
        "*Invoke-Expression*",
        "*DownloadString*",
        "*DownloadFile*",
        "*WebClient*",
        "*Invoke-WebRequest*",
        "*Net.WebClient*",
        "*Start-BitsTransfer*"
    )
)
| eval detection_name="Suspicious PowerShell Execution"
| eval mitre_technique="T1059.001"
| eval severity="High"
| eval analyst_note=case(
    like(CommandLine, "%-EncodedCommand%") OR like(CommandLine, "%-enc %"),
        "PRIORITY: Encoded command - decode and inspect payload immediately",
    like(CommandLine, "%DownloadString%") OR like(CommandLine, "%WebClient%"),
        "PRIORITY: Download cradle detected - check network logs for destination",
    like(CommandLine, "%IEX%") OR like(CommandLine, "%Invoke-Expression%"),
        "PRIORITY: In-memory execution - check script block logs for full payload",
    1=1,
        "Review CommandLine field for suspicious intent"
  )
| table _time, ComputerName, User, Image, ParentImage, CommandLine,
        ProcessId, ParentProcessId, severity, mitre_technique, analyst_note
| sort -_time

================================================================================
SECONDARY SPL QUERY — Suspicious PowerShell Parent Process
================================================================================

| Detection: PowerShell Spawned from Unusual Parent |

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational"
EventID=1
Image IN ("*\\powershell.exe", "*\\pwsh.exe")
ParentImage IN (
    "*\\winword.exe",
    "*\\excel.exe",
    "*\\outlook.exe",
    "*\\mspub.exe",
    "*\\visio.exe",
    "*\\onenote.exe",
    "*\\wscript.exe",
    "*\\cscript.exe",
    "*\\mshta.exe",
    "*\\rundll32.exe",
    "*\\regsvr32.exe",
    "*\\explorer.exe",
    "*\\svchost.exe",
    "*\\taskeng.exe",
    "*\\wmiprvse.exe"
)
| eval detection_name="PowerShell Spawned from Suspicious Parent"
| eval mitre_technique="T1059.001"
| eval severity="High"
| eval risk_score=case(
    ParentImage IN ("*\\winword.exe","*\\excel.exe","*\\outlook.exe"), 90,
    ParentImage IN ("*\\wscript.exe","*\\cscript.exe","*\\mshta.exe"), 85,
    ParentImage IN ("*\\rundll32.exe","*\\regsvr32.exe"), 80,
    1=1, 70
  )
| table _time, ComputerName, User, Image, ParentImage, CommandLine,
        ProcessId, ParentProcessId, risk_score, mitre_technique
| sort -risk_score, -_time

================================================================================
TERTIARY SPL QUERY — Script Block Logging (Event ID 4104)
================================================================================

| Detection: Suspicious Script Block Content |

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational"
EventID=4104
(
    ScriptBlockText IN (
        "*IEX*",
        "*Invoke-Expression*",
        "*Invoke-Mimikatz*",
        "*Invoke-Shellcode*",
        "*Net.WebClient*",
        "*DownloadString*",
        "*DownloadFile*",
        "*FromBase64String*",
        "*[Convert]::FromBase64*",
        "*-bxor*",
        "*Marshal.Copy*",
        "*VirtualAlloc*",
        "*AmsiBypass*",
        "*amsiInitFailed*",
        "*[Ref].Assembly.GetType*"
    )
)
| eval detection_name="Malicious PowerShell Script Block Content"
| eval mitre_technique="T1059.001"
| eval severity="Critical"
| eval analyst_priority=case(
    like(ScriptBlockText, "%Invoke-Mimikatz%"), "CRITICAL - Possible credential theft",
    like(ScriptBlockText, "%AmsiBypass%") OR like(ScriptBlockText, "%amsiInitFailed%"),
        "CRITICAL - AMSI bypass attempt",
    like(ScriptBlockText, "%VirtualAlloc%") OR like(ScriptBlockText, "%Marshal.Copy%"),
        "HIGH - Shellcode loading pattern",
    like(ScriptBlockText, "%FromBase64String%"), "HIGH - Base64 decode in script",
    1=1, "MEDIUM - Review script block content"
  )
| table _time, ComputerName, UserID, ScriptBlockId, ScriptBlockText,
        severity, analyst_priority, mitre_technique
| sort -severity, -_time

================================================================================
COMBINED HIGH-CONFIDENCE QUERY (Recommended for Alerting)
================================================================================

| Use this as your primary alert rule — correlates process + content |

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational"
EventID=1
Image IN ("*\\powershell.exe", "*\\pwsh.exe")
| search
    CommandLine="*-EncodedCommand*" OR
    CommandLine="*-enc *" OR
    CommandLine="*-ExecutionPolicy Bypass*" OR
    (CommandLine="*IEX*" CommandLine="*DownloadString*") OR
    (CommandLine="*WebClient*" CommandLine="*DownloadString*") OR
    ParentImage IN ("*\\winword.exe","*\\excel.exe","*\\wscript.exe","*\\mshta.exe")
| eval detection="Suspicious PowerShell - Combined"
| eval mitre="T1059.001"
| eval severity="High"
| eval triage_priority=case(
    ParentImage IN ("*\\winword.exe","*\\excel.exe"), "P1 - Office macro spawn",
    like(CommandLine, "%-EncodedCommand%"), "P1 - Encoded payload",
    like(CommandLine, "%DownloadString%"), "P2 - Download cradle",
    1=1, "P2 - Review required"
  )
| table _time, ComputerName, User, ParentImage, Image, CommandLine,
        ProcessId, ParentProcessId, triage_priority
| sort -_time

================================================================================
ANALYST TRIAGE STEPS
================================================================================

When this alert fires, execute these steps in order:

STEP 1 — SCOPE THE PROCESS CONTEXT
  Run in Splunk:
    index=endpoint ComputerName=<affected_host> ProcessId=<pid> EventID IN (1,3,11,12,13,23)
    | sort _time
  Purpose: Get the full process timeline — what did this PowerShell process do after it started?

STEP 2 — IDENTIFY THE PARENT
  Check ParentImage and ParentCommandLine.
  Questions:
    - Is the parent a legitimate process (explorer, services, scheduled task)?
    - Is the parent an Office application, script engine, or browser?
    - What is the parent's PID? Look it up in the process chain.

STEP 3 — DECODE ENCODED COMMANDS (if -EncodedCommand detected)
  In PowerShell (on an isolated analysis machine):
    [System.Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetString([System.Convert]::FromBase64String("<base64_string>"))
  Alternatively, use CyberChef (https://gchq.github.io/CyberChef/) with:
    Recipe: From Base64 → Decode Text (UTF-16LE)

STEP 4 — CHECK NETWORK ACTIVITY (if download cradle detected)
  Run in Splunk:
    index=endpoint ComputerName=<affected_host> EventID=3 ProcessId=<pid>
    | table _time, DestinationIp, DestinationPort, DestinationHostname
  Check destination IP/domain against threat intelligence.

STEP 5 — CHECK SCRIPT BLOCK LOGS
    index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational"
    EventID=4104 ComputerName=<affected_host>
    | sort _time
  Reconstruct the full script if it was broken across multiple blocks (join on ScriptBlockId).

STEP 6 — CHECK FOR PERSISTENCE OR FOLLOW-ON ACTIVITY
  Look for:
    - New scheduled tasks (Event ID 4698)
    - Registry Run key changes (Sysmon Event ID 13)
    - New files written to temp/AppData (Sysmon Event ID 11)
    - Network connections to external IPs (Sysmon Event ID 3)

STEP 7 — ASSESS IMPACT AND DECIDE
  Options:
    A) FALSE POSITIVE  → Document why, add exclusion, close alert
    B) TRUE POSITIVE   → Escalate, isolate host, open formal investigation report
    C) INCONCLUSIVE    → Escalate to L2 with full evidence package

================================================================================
KNOWN FALSE POSITIVES
================================================================================

1. IT AUTOMATION SCRIPTS
   Pattern: Administrator accounts running -ExecutionPolicy Bypass with signed scripts
   Handling: Create exclusion for known admin users + signed script paths
   SPL exclusion:
     NOT (User IN ("domain\\it_admin","domain\\svc_deploy") AND
          CommandLine="*-File C:\\Scripts\\*")

2. SOFTWARE DEPLOYMENT TOOLS
   Pattern: SCCM/ConfigMgr, Intune, or third-party tools invoking PowerShell silently
   Handling: Exclude known deployment service accounts and executable paths
   SPL exclusion:
     NOT (ParentImage IN ("*\\ccmexec.exe","*\\ServiceUI.exe"))

3. WINDOWS UPDATE / PATCHING
   Pattern: PowerShell invoked by TrustedInstaller or SYSTEM during patching windows
   Handling: Create time-based exclusion during maintenance windows
   SPL exclusion:
     NOT (User="NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM" AND
          ParentImage="*\\TrustedInstaller.exe")

4. DEVELOPER TOOLS
   Pattern: VS Code, Visual Studio, JetBrains running PowerShell with bypass during testing
   Handling: Exclude known developer workstations by hostname or OU
   SPL exclusion:
     NOT ComputerName IN ("DEV-WS-001","DEV-WS-002")

================================================================================
TUNING RECOMMENDATIONS
================================================================================

Priority 1 (Do First):
  - Build a whitelist of known admin service accounts and exclude them
  - Exclude SYSTEM-context PowerShell from patching/maintenance processes
  - Verify script block logging is enabled — without it, DET-001c is blind

Priority 2 (After Baseline):
  - Run the query in search mode for 72 hours before enabling as an alert
  - Document every false positive hit with the triggering CommandLine
  - Build a lookup table of approved encoded commands (scheduled tasks, etc.)

Priority 3 (Advanced Tuning):
  - Add risk scoring by combining parent process + commandline flags
  - Correlate with Sysmon Event ID 3 (network) to prioritize download cradles
  - Add lookup against threat intel for known-bad PowerShell C2 strings

================================================================================
RESPONSE RECOMMENDATION
================================================================================

TRUE POSITIVE RESPONSE:
  Immediate (0-15 min):
    1. Isolate affected endpoint from network
    2. Preserve memory image if possible (before reboot)
    3. Pull full Splunk timeline for affected host
    4. Notify SOC lead and open P1 incident ticket

  Short-term (15-60 min):
    5. Decode all encoded commands found
    6. Check for persistence mechanisms (scheduled tasks, run keys, services)
    7. Identify lateral movement from affected host (Type 3 logons outbound)
    8. Check for data staging or exfiltration indicators

  Recovery:
    9. Rebuild endpoint from known-good image if compromise confirmed
   10. Reset credentials for affected user accounts
   11. Review AD for new accounts or group membership changes

================================================================================
LAB SIMULATION REFERENCE
================================================================================

To generate telemetry for this detection in the lab:

  Safe simulation command (Windows 10 lab VM only):
    powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -EncodedCommand
    V3JpdGUtSG9zdCAiTGFiIHRlc3QgLSBub3QgbWFsaWNpb3Vz"

  The above decodes to: Write-Host "Lab test - not malicious"

  This will trigger DET-001a (command line flags) without doing anything harmful.

  See full simulation: attack-simulations/01-powershell-suspicious-execution.md
